    They are typically used to manage the data of a Gst.Buffer.

    A GstMemory object has an allocated region of memory of maxsize. The maximum
    size does not change during the lifetime of the memory object. The memory
    also has an offset and size property that specifies the valid range of memory
    in the allocated region.

    Memory is usually created by allocators with a Gst.Allocator.alloc()
    method call. When None is used as the allocator, the default allocator will
    be used.

    New allocators can be registered with Gst.Allocator.register().
    Allocators are identified by name and can be retrieved with
    Gst.Allocator.find(). Gst.Allocator.set_default() can be used to change the
    default allocator.

    New memory can be created with Gst.Memory.new_wrapped() that wraps the memory
    allocated elsewhere.

    Refcounting of the memory block is performed with gst_memory_ref() and
    gst_memory_unref().

    The size of the memory can be retrieved and changed with
    Gst.Memory.get_sizes() and Gst.Memory.resize() respectively.

    Getting access to the data of the memory is performed with Gst.Memory.map().
    The call will return a pointer to offset bytes into the region of memory.
    After the memory access is completed, Gst.Memory.unmap() should be called.

    Memory can be copied with Gst.Memory.copy(), which will return a writable
    copy. Gst.Memory.share() will create a new memory block that shares the
    memory with an existing memory block at a custom offset and with a custom
    size.

    Memory can be efficiently merged when Gst.Memory.is_span() returns True.
    """
    size: _int
    offset: _int
    align: _int
    maxsize: _int
    parent: Memory
    allocator: Allocator
    mini_object: MiniObject
    def copy(self, offset: _int, size: _int) -> Memory | None:
        """Return a copy of size bytes from self starting from offset. This copy is
        guaranteed to be writable. size can be set to -1 to return a copy
        from offset to the end of the memory region.

        Args:
            offset: offset to copy from
            size: size to copy, or -1 to copy to the end of the memory region

        Returns:
            a new copy of self if the copy succeeded, None otherwise.
        """
    def get_sizes(self) -> _tuple[_int, _int, _int]:
        """Get the current size, offset and maxsize of self.

        Returns:
            the current size of self
            offset: pointer to offset
            maxsize: pointer to maxsize
        """
    def is_span(self, mem2: Memory) -> _tuple[_bool, _int]:
        """Check if self and mem2 share the memory with a common parent memory object
        and that the memory is contiguous.

        If this is the case, the memory of self and mem2 can be merged
        efficiently by performing Gst.Memory.share() on the parent object from
        the returned offset.

        Args:
            mem2: a Gst.Memory

        Returns:
            True if the memory is contiguous and of a common parent.
            offset: a pointer to a result offset
        """
    def is_type(self, mem_type: _str) -> _bool:
        """Check if self if allocated with an allocator for mem_type.

        Args:
            mem_type: a memory type

        Returns:
            True if self was allocated from an allocator for mem_type.
        """
    def make_mapped(self, flags: MapFlags) -> _tuple[Memory | None, MapInfo]:
        """Create a Gst.Memory object that is mapped with flags. If self is mappable
        with flags, this function returns the mapped self directly. Otherwise a
        mapped copy of self is returned.

        This function takes ownership of old self and returns a reference to a new
        Gst.Memory.

        Args:
            flags: mapping flags

        Returns:
            a Gst.Memory object mapped
                with flags or None when a mapping is not possible.
            info: pointer for info
        """
    def map(self, flags: MapFlags) -> _tuple[_bool, MapInfo]:
        """Fill info with the pointer and sizes of the memory in self that can be
        accessed according to flags.

        This function can return False for various reasons:
        - the memory backed by self is not accessible with the given flags.
        - the memory was already mapped with a different mapping.

